
Kotasoft EP128pal - IMAGE CONVERTER PROGRAM FOR ENTERPRISE 128 COMPUTERS

The aim of the program is to convert images in format using the available colours of the Enterprise computer. The target is not to reach the maximum graphic ability, it is rather a help in developing Enterprise programs, e.g. creating background for games, menus which can be shown on Enterprise. The supported video modes are: 4 colours, 16 colours and 256 colours, and there is a function to find the 'best' palette in 4 or 16 colours mode. In 16 colours mode the BIAS is handled. The converted image can be saved with .EPI extension which can be loaded and displayed on Enterprise computer.
The 2 colours mode, low resolution mode is not supported by this program, but the different palettes in each rows are supported.

An extra function is to convert images using the colours of the Commodore 64 computer. The image can be also saved in .EPI file in this format but the palette is not saved because of the complexity how the BIAS works.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------


MAIN FUNCTIONS ON THE SCREEN


o Buttons on the top

o Screen settings

o Palette finenesses

o Other settings

o Palette


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------


BUTTONS ON THE TOP


o Convert image: Convert the read .BMP or .JPG image according to the settings.

o Open: Read a .BMP or .JPG image.

o Save: Save the converted image in .EPI or .BMP format.

o Character editor: Start character editor. Described below.

o Unpack: Unpack the packed image.
	If the selected image is not packed it will be packed instead of unpacking.

o Exit: Save the palette and settings in file EP128PAL.CNF, then exit.


Progress bar: It shows the process of the converting. The colours of the progress bar indicates the following phases:
	blue - creating work bitmap from the visible part of the original image, mixing pixels
	green - generating palettes
	gray - extending palette colours from the adjacent palettes
	red - generating pixels, dithering


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------


SCREEN SETTINGS


Number of colours

o 4 colours: Use 4 colurs mode.

o 16 colours: Use 16 colours mode, colours 0-7 are freely selectable, remaining colours are defined by the BIAS.

o 256 colours: Use all the 256 colours.

o Attribute mode: Use 16 colours attribute mode.

o C64 palette: Use the colours of Commodore 64.


Dimensions

o X: The horizontal size of the converted image in characters. The value must be between 4 and 46.

o Y: The vertical size of the converted image in characters. The value must be between 1 and 28.

o Screen resolution: The real resolution of the image, as we will see on Enterprise. It is calculated.
	The horizontal resolution depends on the number of colours, e.g. if 16 colours are used it is four times the value.
	The vertical resolution is nine times the value.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------


PALETTE FINENESSES


o Save palette: Save the converted or manual created palette and settings in .CNF file.

o Load palette: Load the palette and settings from .CNF file.

o Generate ideal palette: The program tries to find that colours of Enterprise which present the best looking of the image. This function is available in 4 and 16 colours mode. In 16 colours mode the BIAS is used and the target is to find that group of colours which contains most number of the needed colours. It is indicated in the information line.

o Create gradient: Create gradient between the two selected colours. The range in the palette can be selected (0-3 in 4 colours mode, 0-7 in 16 colours mode).


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------


OTHER SETTINGS


[x] Resize image
	You can choose between resizing the .BMP or .JPG image to fit the EP screen size or using a part of the image in original size.

	[x] Proportional
		When the image is resized it keeps the aspect ratio if this option is checked, otherwise the size will be stretched or shrinked to selected image size both horizontally and vertically.

	[x] Full fill
		If it is checked and the image is resized the whole converted image area will be filled. If the original and converted image ratios are different the edge of the original image will be out of the converted area.
		If it is unchecked and the image is resized the whole original image will be seen. If the original and converted image ratios are different the remaining area will be black.

[x] Save as Enterprise image
	Define that the image will be saved in .BMP or .EPI format. If you check it .EPI file will be created for Enterprise usage.

[x] Include palettes
	If this option is checked the palettes will be saved in the image. This works only in 4 and 16 colours modes, in 256 colours mode and C64 colours mode this function is not available.

[x] Pack
	Beside the normal save the image will also be saved as a packed .EPZ file.

[x] Method of colour searching
	Defines how the RGB components will be calculated when the program searches the colour of the pixels.
	Possible choices are: simple, weighted and exponential.

[x] Weight pixels on the edge (attr)
	Weight the pixels on the edge in the 8 pixels parts in attribute mode. It is available only if different palettes in each pixel lines are used.

[x] Mix pixels
	If the source image is larger than the converted image we have more pixels to create the converted one.
	Unchecked: one pixel is created by using one pixel from the original image.
	Checked: all the covered pixels are used in calculatig the generated pixel, it calculates average from the RGB components.

[x] Dithering
	Create colour shades by using generated noise in the image.
	Unchecked: the most similar colour to the pixel will be used.
	Checked: a pattern will be used with the two most similar colours, depending on the shade.

	Max. difference: when shades are dithered the difference of the pixel colours can be maximized by the trackbar.

	[x] Floyd-Steinberg dithering algorithm
		Use the algorithm published by Robert W. Floyd and Louis Steinberg in 1976.

[x] Generate ideal
	Generate (or do not generate) palette at converting automatically.
	If you would like to create palette manually this option must be unchecked, otherwise your palette will be overwritten. You can create your own palette and the converted image will use the colours of this palette. These colours will be saved automatically when you exit from the program, and you also can save the colours in a .CNF file.

[x] Keep BIAS
	If it is checked the BIAS colours will be kept when the palette is generated.

[x] Use most frequent colours
	The method of colour usage at converting. If checked the most frequent colours will be used.
	Most frequent colours: those colours which exist in a block in the largest number.
		Example: in a picture of grass the green shades dominate, use as much green colours as possible.
	Different colours: use as different shades as possible.
		Example: give red shade for the small ladybird in the dominant grass.
	When the option is unchecked it can be chosen by the trackbar which method is more dominant.
	The result depends on the image content, you can play with it.

[x] Colour 0 is always this:
	If it is checked the colour 0 in the palette will always be the selected colour when the palette is generated.
	Impact on searching for the best BIAS: when the colours are counted the selected one will not calculated as that colour will be in the palette on position 0.

[x] Combine colours of line pairs
	It can be used if different palettes in each pixel lines are used. If it is set on the colours in palettes of two consecutive lines are combined and put in the two lines alternately which gives different result. It can be better in 16 colours mode.


Note: There is an information line under the palette which can show the result of some functions, e.g. position of the mouse click, number of colour matches when searching the best BIAS.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------


PALETTE


o 4 colours
	4 colours palette. You can change a colour by clicking on it to the selected colour in the 256 colours palette.

o 16 colours
	16 colours palette. You can change a colour by clicking on it to the selected colour in the 256 colours palette.
	If you click on the BIAS part you can choose that 8 colours group which is specified in the 256 colours palette.

o 256 colours
	The colours of the Enterprise. You can select one colour by clicking.


Statistics: By right clicking on the 4 or 16 colours palette this function counts the pixels with the same colour as the selected one. These pixels are shown on the generated image while the right button is kept pressed.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------


TIP

After some experience we can get acceptable result with these settings in 16 colour and attribute modes:
 - different palettes in each character lines
 - the 'Use most requent colours' is unchecked, the trackbar is about in middle position
 - 42x28 resolution
 - 'Dithering' is checked, max. difference is 40
 - 'Floyd-Steinberg dithering algorithm' is checked
 - 'Mix pixels' is checked
 - open not too large size .BMP or .JPG image 

Naturally the result of the converting depends on the image content beside the settings. It is recommended to try more settings. Sometimes we can get better result by palette created manually.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------


IMAGES


Original image: The loaded .BMP or .JPG image is displayed here.

o Pick up colour: You can select the closest shade from the Enterprise colours by clicking on the image.

o Scroll: If the resize is turned off and the image is larger than the size described by the resolution settings the scroll is available by the mouse, keeping the left button pressed.

o Paste: Image from the clipboard can be pasted by right click.

o Blocks on the left side of the image: Here you can see how the image is splitted into blocks. The default is one screen is one block with one palette but you can change it. One block is continuous till it is indicated by the same (white or blue) colour. Where the colour changes a new block starts. The blocks can be any size, the image can be splitted into 27 blocks maximum with the height of one character line. 
	Simple click: It inverts all boxes below the given block, you can use it to split or merge blocks.
	Double click: A simple way to toggle between 1 and 27 blocks mode.
	Right click: Change into mode of different palettes in each pixel lines.

o Video modes: The video mode of the block is shown left to the blocks. The video mode can be rotated by simple click. The order is: 4, 16, 256 colours, attribute mode and C64 colours.


Converted image: You can see the result of the converting here.

o Zoom: Double click on the image to show the it in full screen mode.

o Choose palette: You can choose the palette of the block by clicking its box at the left side of the image. You can see the colours of the palette in 4 and 16 colours mode. The palette of a line is also can be selected by simple click on the converted image.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------


THE FORMAT OF THE .EPI FILE


It has two parts: the header and the blocks. The blocks contain the palette and the bitmap.

Header: Image information. The structure:

01-08. (8 bytes): the version number of EP128pal which was used to save the image
09-09. (1 byte): vertical resolution of the full image
10-10. (1 byte): the size of one physical line in bytes
	This value shows how much bytes are required for one line in the video memory.
	In 40 characters wide screen it is 80 bytes.
	In attribute mode it contains the size of both the bitmap and attribute data.
11-11. (1 byte): number of blocks
12-12. (1 byte): a value of the BIAS (it is always saved)


Blocks: It contains the palette (if saved), the number of lines and the bitmap.

The palette is saved only if the checkbox was checked. The palette takes 4 bytes in 4 colours mode and 8 bytes in 16 colours mode. The palette is not saved in 256 colours mode or when the Commodore 64 colours are used. If different palettes are used in each pixel lines a 4 or 8 bytes long palette will be saved before every bitmap.

The structure of the block:

01-01. (1 byte): video mode
	50: 4 colours mode
	82: 16 colours mode
	114: 256 colours mode
	20: attribute mode
	0: 16 colours mode with Commodore 64 colours
02-02. (1 byte): number of graphic lines in the block
03-03. (1 byte): different palettes in each pixel lines, palettes are saved
	Bit 0: It is set on if the different palettes in each pixel lines are used.
	Bit 1: It is set on if the palettes are saved in the blocks.
04-07/11. (4 or 8 bytes): palette
08/12-xx. bitmap


The bitmap contains the pixels line by line. The length of the line depends on the resolution, it is maximum 84 bytes. The information kept on one byte:
	In 4 colours mode: 4 pixels
	In 16 colours mode: 2 pixels
	In 256 colours mode: 1 pixels
	In attribute mode: 4 pixels (the attribute data are first, the bitmap is next)


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------


THE METHOD OF PACK


When the file is packed the repeating characters are stored in a shorter form. The format of the packed (.EPZ) file:

o Header: 5 bytes
	01-04. (4 bytes): the length of the .EPZ file in bytes
	05-05. (1 byte): always a 'Z' character

o Data:
	06-06. (1 byte): the size of following data in bytes
	07-	a stored / packed data
	...

The size of the following data indicates how much characters are stored. The maximum value is 127 and the bit 7 indicates that the data is packed or not.
	Bit 7 = 0: Unpacked bytes are coming. The bits 0-6 shows how much bytes can be read.
	Bit 7 = 1: Packed bytes are coming. The bits 0-6 shows how much times the next byte must be repeated.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------


CHARACTER EDITOR


Simple character editor to display text on graphic screen. The highest supported resolution is 8x12 pixels, the smallest characters are 3x3 pixels. Only the used characters are saved, this solution saves memory when small number of characters are used. The characters are monochrome, the display effects are just examples to show how the text can be displayed.


Parts of the screen:

- Settings

	o Character code: The code of the selected character can be changed. Only that code is allowed which is not used yet.
	o X size: The horizontal size of the characters, valid values are between 3 and 8.
	o Y size: The vertical size of the characters, valid values are between 3 and 12.
	o Spacing: Space between the characters in pixels, valid values are between 0 and 10.

	o Background: Variable background colour (not saved in the .EPF file).
	o Colour 1: Variable primary colour (not saved in the .EPF file).
	o Colour 2: Variable secondary colour (not saved in the .EPF file).

	o Standard writing: Writing with one colour (not saved in the .EPF file).
	o Shadow writing: Writing with two colours (not saved in the .EPF file).
	o Bicolour (split horizontally): Writing with two colours split horizontally (not saved in the .EPF file).
	o Bicolour (split vertically): Writing with two colours split vertically (not saved in the .EPF file).

	o Number of characters: The value can be between 1 and 128, it shows the number of the used characters.

- Characters

	The table of characters. A character can be selected by a mouse click.
	In the upper line the characters are shown in normal size, without zoom.

- Editor

	The selected character can be edited by the mouse. The pixels can be inverted by clicking.

- Buttons

	o Insert: Insert a character before the selected character.
	o Delete: Delete the selected character.
	o Sort by code: The characters will be ordered by character code.
	o Open: Load .EPF character file.
	o Save: Save the edited characters in .EPF file.
	o <<<< Shift left: The pixels of the selected character will be shifted left.
	o Shift right >>>>: The pixels of the selected character will be shifted right.
	o ^^ Shift up ^^: The pixels of the selected character will be shifted up.
	o vv Shift down vv: The pixels of the selected character will be shifted down.
	o Copy: The selected character will be copied to another character. The copy can be done while the button is pink (8 seconds).
	o Invert: The pixels of the selected character will be inverted.
	o Empty: The pixels of the selected character will be cleared.
	o Back: Back to the image converter function.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------


THE FORMAT OF THE .EPF FILE


01-01. (1 byte): number of characters
02-02. (1 byte): horizontal size of the characteres in pixels
03-03. (1 byte): vertical size of the characteres in pixels
04-04. (1 byte): space between the characters in pixels
05-05. (1 byte): the code of the first character
06-aa. (x bytes): the bitmap of the first character (one byte represents one line)
bb-bb. (1 byte): the code of the second character
cc-dd. (x bytes): the bitmap of the second character
...


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------


IS-BASIC EXAMPLE PROGRAM TO READ THE .EPI FILE


This program is very slow, but it shows how to display the .EPI images.
(The lines from number 3200 are not executed because the bitmap is loaded directly into the video memory by the SPOKE statement.)


100 PROGRAM "KEP.BAS"
110 !
120 ! Definiciok
130 !
140 STRING PUFFER$*128
150 NUMERIC LPTADDR,LINE1ST,VSEG,VOFS,VADDR,VLINE,VROW,XBYTE
160 NUMERIC PUFOLVASOTT,SZINEK,FY,PAL(0 TO 8),YPOZ,BLOKKOK,PALETTA,BIAS,PIXELSOROK
170 !
180 LET LPTADDR=SPEEK(255,16372)+SPEEK(255,16373)*256-32768
190 LET LINE1ST=LPTADDR+16+4
200 INPUT PROMPT "Kep neve: ":F$
210 IF POS(F$,".")=0 THEN LET F$=F$&".EPI"
300 !
310 ! Fejlec beolvasasa
320 !
330 SET BORDER YELLOW
340 CALL PUFURIT
350 OPEN #50:F$ ACCESS INPUT
360 CALL OLVAS(12)
370 LET FY=SZAM(9,1)
380 LET XBYTE=SZAM(10,1)
390 LET BLOKKOK=SZAM(11,1)
400 LET BIAS=SZAM(12,1)
500 !
510 ! Blokkok feldolgozasa
520 !
530 LET YPOZ=1
540 SET BORDER RED
550 FOR BLOKK=1 TO BLOKKOK
560   CALL FELDOLGOZ
570 NEXT
580 CLOSE #50
700 !
710 ! Varakozas, kilepes
720 !
730 SET BORDER BLACK
740 PING
750 DO
760   GET C$
770 LOOP UNTIL C$=""
780 DO
790   GET C$
800 LOOP UNTIL C$<>""
810 FOR BLOKK=BLOKKOK TO 1 STEP -1
820   CLOSE #BLOKK
830 NEXT BLOKK
840 TEXT 40
850 END
1000 !
1010 ! Beolvas DARAB karaktert
1020 !
1030 DEF OLVAS(DARAB)
1040   STRING C$*1
1050   LET I=0
1060   DO
1070     GET #50:C$
1080     IF C$<>"" THEN
1090       LET PUFFER$=PUFFER$&C$
1100       LET I=I+1
1110     END IF
1120   LOOP WHILE I<DARAB AND C$<>""
1130   LET PUFOLVASOTT=PUFOLVASOTT+I
1140 END DEF
1200 !
1210 ! Puffer kiuritese
1220 !
1230 DEF PUFURIT
1240   LET PUFFER$=""
1250   LET PUFOLVASOTT=0
1260 END DEF
1300 !
1310 ! Atalakit numerikussa
1320 !
1330 DEF SZAM(POZ,HOSSZ)
1340   STRING C$*1
1350   NUMERIC SZAMOL
1360   LET I=1
1370   LET SZOROZ=1
1380   LET SZAMOL=0
1390   DO WHILE I<=HOSSZ
1400     LET SZAMOL=SZAMOL+ORD(PUFFER$(POZ+I-1))*SZOROZ
1410     LET I=I+1
1420     LET SZOROZ=SZOROZ*256
1430   LOOP
1440   LET SZAM=SZAMOL
1450 END DEF
2000 !
2010 ! Egy blokk feldolgozasa
2020 !
2030 DEF FELDOLGOZ
2040   CALL PUFURIT
2050   ! Blokk fejlece
2060   CALL OLVAS(3)
2070   LET VIDEOMOD=SZAM(1,1)
2080   LET SOROK=SZAM(2,1)
2090   LET PIXELSOROK=(SZAM(3,1) BAND 1)>0
2100   LET PALETTA=(SZAM(3,1) BAND 2)>0
2110   IF PIXELSOROK OR VIDEOMOD=20 OR VIDEOMOD=0 OR XBYTE>84 OR FY>243 OR SOROK/9<>INT(SOROK/9) THEN
2120     TEXT 40
2130     PRINT "Ez az uzemmod nem tamogatott."
2140     CLOSE #50
2150     END
2160   END IF
2170   ! Paletta beolvasasa
2180   IF PALETTA THEN
2190     CALL PUFURIT
2200     IF VIDEOMOD=50 THEN
2210       CALL OLVAS(4)
2220     ELSE
2230       CALL OLVAS(8)
2240     END IF
2250   END IF
2260   FOR SOR=1 TO SOROK
2270     IF SOR=1 THEN
2280       SET BORDER BLUE
2290       ! Videolap nyitasa
2300       SET VIDEO MODE 1
2310       SELECT CASE VIDEOMOD
2320       CASE 50
2330         SET VIDEO COLOUR 1
2340       CASE 82
2350         SET VIDEO COLOUR 2
2360       CASE ELSE
2370         SET VIDEO COLOUR 3
2380       END SELECT
2390       SET VIDEO X XBYTE/2
2400       SET VIDEO Y SOROK/9
2410       OPEN #BLOKK:"VIDEO:"
2420       DISPLAY #BLOKK:AT YPOZ FROM 1 TO SOROK/9
2430       ! Paletta beallitasa
2440       IF VIDEOMOD=50 THEN
2450         SET #BLOKK:PALETTE SZAM(1,1),SZAM(2,1),SZAM(3,1),SZAM(4,1)
2460       END IF
2470       IF VIDEOMOD=82 THEN
2480         SET #BLOKK:PALETTE SZAM(1,1),SZAM(2,1),SZAM(3,1),SZAM(4,1),SZAM(5,1),SZAM(6,1),SZAM(7,1),SZAM(8,1)
2490         SET BIAS BIAS
2500       END IF
2510     END IF
2520     ! Bitterkep beolvasasa
2530     SET BORDER SOR BAND 255
2540     CALL VIDEOADDR(0,(YPOZ-1)*9+SOR-1)
2550     CALL PUFURIT
2560     CALL OLVAS(XBYTE)
2570     FOR X=1 TO XBYTE
2580       SPOKE VSEG,VOFS,ORD(PUFFER$(X))
2590       LET VOFS=VOFS+1
2600       IF VOFS>16383 THEN
2610         LET VSEG=VSEG+1:LET VOFS=0
2620         IF VSEG>255 THEN LET VSEG=252
2630       END IF
2640     NEXT X
2650   NEXT YY
2660   LET YPOZ=YPOZ+SOROK/9
2670 END DEF 
3000 !
3010 ! Szegmens es ofszet kiszamitasa (a teljes kepernyot
3020 ! osszefuggoen kezeli, PY-bol szamolja az EXOS sor szamat)
3030 !   PXB ..... vizszintes pozicio (bajtban, nem pixelben)
3040 !   PY ...... fuggoleges pozicio (teljes kepernyo)
3050 !   VLINE ... a sor melyik EXOS sorhoz tartozik
3060 !   VROW .... a sor hol van az adott EXOS soron belul
3070 !   VADDR ... Nick cim
3080 !   VSEG .... a Nick cimbol kiszamolt szegmens
3090 !   VOFS .... a Nick cimbol kiszamolt ofszet
3100 !
3110 DEF VIDEOADDR(PXB,PY)
3120   LET VLINE=INT(PY/9)
3130   LET VROW=PY-VLINE*9
3140   LET VADDR=SPEEK(255,LINE1ST+VLINE*16)+SPEEK(255,LINE1ST+1+VLINE*16)*256+VROW*XBYTE+PXB
3150   LET VSEG=252+INT(VADDR/16384)
3160   LET VOFS=VADDR-INT(VADDR/16384)*16384
3170 END DEF


3200 !
3210 ! Keppont rajzolasa 256 szinu modban
3220 !
3230 DEF PUTPIXEL256(X,Y,COLOUR)
3240   CALL VIDEOADDR(X,Y)
3250   SPOKE VSEG,VOFS,COLOUR
3260 END DEF
3300 !
3310 ! Keppont rajzolasa 2 szinu modban
3320 !
3330 DEF PUTPIXEL2(X,Y,COLOUR)
3340   CALL VIDEOADDR(INT(X/8),Y)
3350   LET H=7-(X BAND 7)
3360   LET PIXEL=SPEEK(VSEG,VOFS) BAND (255-2^H)
3370   SPOKE VSEG,VOFS,PIXEL BOR COLOUR*2^H
3380 END DEF
3500 !
3510 ! Keppont rajzolasa 4 szinu modban
3520 !
3530 DEF PUTPIXEL4(X,Y,COLOUR)
3540   CALL VIDEOADDR(INT(X/4),Y)
3550   LET H=3-(X BAND 3)
3560   IF H=3 THEN
3570     LET PIXEL=SPEEK(VSEG,VOFS) BAND 119
3580     LET COL=(COLOUR BAND 1)*8+(COLOUR BAND 2)*64
3590   END IF
3600   IF H=2 THEN
3610     LET PIXEL=SPEEK(VSEG,VOFS) BAND 187
3620     LET COL=(COLOUR BAND 1)*4+(COLOUR BAND 2)*32
3630   END IF
3640   IF H=1 THEN
3650     LET PIXEL=SPEEK(VSEG,VOFS) BAND 221
3660     LET COL=(COLOUR BAND 1)*2+(COLOUR BAND 2)*16
3670   END IF
3680   IF H=0 THEN
3690     LET PIXEL=SPEEK(VSEG,VOFS) BAND 238
3700     LET COL=(COLOUR BAND 1)+(COLOUR BAND 2)*8
3710   END IF
3720   SPOKE VSEG,VOFS,PIXEL BOR COL
3730 END DEF
3800 !
3810 ! Keppont rajzolasa 16 szinu modban
3820 !
3830 DEF PUTPIXEL16(X,Y,COLOUR)
3840   CALL VIDEOADDR(INT(X/2),Y)
3850   IF (X BAND 1)=0 THEN
3860     LET PIXEL=SPEEK(VSEG,VOFS) BAND 85
3870     LET COL=(COLOUR BAND 1)*128+(COLOUR BAND 4)*8+(COLOUR BAND 2)*4+INT((COLOUR BAND 8)/4)
3880   ELSE
3890     LET PIXEL=SPEEK(VSEG,VOFS) BAND 170
3900     LET COL=(COLOUR BAND 1)*64+(COLOUR BAND 4)*4+(COLOUR BAND 2)*2+INT((COLOUR BAND 8)/8)
3910   END IF
3920   SPOKE VSEG,VOFS,PIXEL BOR COL
3930 END DEF


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------


HISOFT PASCAL EXAMPLE PROGRAM TO READ THE .EPI FILE


This program also shows how to read a saved image.
Note: before compiling the size of stacks must be set to value C000h by 'Alter' command which is needed for using the page 3.


PROGRAM Kep;

{$L- }
{$O- }
{$A+ }
{$S+ }
{$I+ }

{ Alter: Translate Stack=C000h, Compiler Stack=C000h, then page 03 can be used }

CONST StrMax=40;

TYPE

PicType=RECORD
 YSize,XByte,Blocks,BIASCol,VideoMode,Lines: CHAR;
 Pal,PixelLines: BOOLEAN;
END;

String=RECORD
 Len: CHAR;
 Txt: ARRAY[1..StrMax] OF CHAR;
END;

VAR

SaveLPTAddr,LPBAddr,SegLimit,VOfs: INTEGER;
VSeg1,VSeg2,VSeg,SegNum: CHAR;
Pic: PicType;
Buf: ARRAY[0..255] OF CHAR;
FileName: String;

PROCEDURE StrSetLen(VAR S0: String);
VAR StrP: INTEGER;
BEGIN
 StrP:=StrMax;
 WHILE (StrP>0) AND ((S0.Txt[StrP]=CHR(0)) OR (S0.Txt[StrP]=CHR(32))) DO StrP:=StrP-1;
 S0.Len:=CHR(StrP);
END;

FUNCTION StrPos(C0: CHAR; S0: String): INTEGER;
VAR StrP: INTEGER;
BEGIN
 StrP:=1;
 WHILE (StrP<=ORD(S0.Len)) AND (S0.Txt[StrP]<>C0) DO StrP:=StrP+1;
 IF StrP<=ORD(S0.Len) THEN StrPos:=StrP ELSE StrPos:=0;
END;

PROCEDURE StrAddChrs(VAR S0: String; C1,C2,C3,C4: CHAR);
BEGIN
 S0.Txt[ORD(S0.Len)+1]:=C1;
 S0.Txt[ORD(S0.Len)+2]:=C2;
 S0.Txt[ORD(S0.Len)+3]:=C3;
 S0.Txt[ORD(S0.Len)+4]:=C4;
 StrSetLen(S0);
END;

PROCEDURE SetVar(Var0,Value0: INTEGER);
{ 27-border,26-status,7-click,8-stop; 0=on,255=off }
BEGIN
 INLINE(#06,#01);     { LD B,1 }
 INLINE(#DD,#4E,#04); { LD C,(IX+04) }
 INLINE(#DD,#56,#02); { LD D,(IX+02) }
 INLINE(#F7,#10);     { EXOS 16 }
END;

PROCEDURE SetBIAS(C0: INTEGER);
BEGIN
 INLINE(#01,#1C,#01); { LD BC,011CH }
 INLINE(#DD,#56,#02); { LD D,(IX+2) }
 INLINE(#F7,#10);     { EXOS 16 }
END;

PROCEDURE EXOS1OpenChn(Chn0,FilePtr0: INTEGER);
VAR Result: CHAR;
BEGIN
 RA:=CHR(Chn0);
 RDE:=FilePtr0;
 Result:=EXOS(1);
END;

PROCEDURE EXOS3CloseChn(Chn0: INTEGER);
VAR Result: CHAR;
BEGIN
 RA:=CHR(Chn0);
 Result:=EXOS(3);
END;

FUNCTION EXOS5GetChar(Chn0: INTEGER): CHAR;
VAR Result: CHAR;
BEGIN
 RA:=CHR(Chn0);
 Result:=EXOS(5);
 EXOS5GetChar:=RB;
END;

PROCEDURE EXOS6GetBlock(Chn0,Bytes0,BufPtr0: INTEGER);
VAR Result: CHAR;
BEGIN
 RA:=CHR(Chn0);
 RBC:=Bytes0;
 RDE:=BufPtr0;
 Result:=EXOS(6);
END;

FUNCTION EXOS24GetSeg: BOOLEAN;
VAR Result: CHAR;
BEGIN
 Result:=EXOS(24);
 SegNum:=RC;
 IF RA=CHR(#7F) THEN SegLimit:=RDE ELSE SegLimit:=#3FFF;
 EXOS24GetSeg:=(RA=CHR(0)) OR (RA=CHR(#7F));
END;

PROCEDURE EXOS25FreeSeg(SegNum0: CHAR);
VAR Result: CHAR;
BEGIN
 RC:=SegNum0;
 Result:=EXOS(25);
END;

FUNCTION GetVideoSeg: BOOLEAN;
VAR SegPtr: INTEGER;
    OK: BOOLEAN;
BEGIN
 VSeg1:=CHR(0); VSeg2:=CHR(0); SegPtr:=0;
 REPEAT
  OK:=EXOS24GetSeg;
  IF OK THEN
   BEGIN
    IF ORD(SegNum)>=#FC THEN
     IF (VSeg1=CHR(0)) AND (SegLimit=#3FFF) THEN VSeg1:=SegNum ELSE VSeg2:=SegNum
    ELSE
     BEGIN
      SegPtr:=SegPtr+1; Buf[SegPtr]:=SegNum;
     END;
   END;
 UNTIL (VSeg2<>CHR(0)) OR (SegPtr=255) OR NOT OK;
 WHILE SegPtr>0 DO
  BEGIN
   EXOS25FreeSeg(Buf[SegPtr]);
   SegPtr:=SegPtr-1;
  END;
 GetVideoSeg:=(VSeg2<>CHR(0));
END;

PROCEDURE SetLPTAddr(NickAddr0: INTEGER);
VAR LPL,LPH: CHAR;
BEGIN
 LPL:=CHR(ORD(PEEK(ADDR(NickAddr0),CHAR)) DIV 16+ORD(PEEK(ADDR(NickAddr0)+1,CHAR)) MOD 16*16);
 LPH:=CHR(ORD(PEEK(ADDR(NickAddr0)+1,CHAR)) DIV 16+#C0);
 OUT(#82,LPL);
 OUT(#83,LPH);
END;

PROCEDURE AddLPB;
VAR i0: INTEGER;
BEGIN
 OUT(#B3,VSeg1);
 FOR i0:=0 TO 15 DO POKE(LPBAddr+i0,Buf[i0]);
 LPBAddr:=LPBAddr+16;
END;

PROCEDURE PicLoad;
VAR Block,X0,Y0,NickAddr0: INTEGER;
    MB0: CHAR;
BEGIN
 { Keep space for LPT }
 VOfs:=4144;
 VSeg:=VSeg1;
 { Open channel, load header }
 EXOS1OpenChn(40,ADDR(FileName));
 EXOS6GetBlock(40,12,ADDR(Buf));
 Pic.YSize:=Buf[8];
 Pic.XByte:=Buf[9];
 Pic.Blocks:=Buf[10];
 Pic.BIASCol:=Buf[11];
 SetBIAS(ORD(Pic.BIASCol));
 { Build LPT, create 27 empty lines }
 LPBAddr:=#C000;
 Buf[4]:=CHR(0);Buf[5]:=CHR(0);Buf[6]:=CHR(0);Buf[7]:=CHR(0);
 Buf[8]:=CHR(0);Buf[9]:=CHR(0);Buf[10]:=CHR(0);Buf[11]:=CHR(0);Buf[12]:=CHR(0);Buf[13]:=CHR(0);Buf[14]:=CHR(0);Buf[15]:=CHR(0);
 Buf[0]:=CHR(229);Buf[1]:=CHR(18);Buf[2]:=CHR(63);Buf[3]:=CHR(0); AddLPB;
 { Process blocks }
 FOR Block:=1 TO ORD(Pic.Blocks) DO
  BEGIN
   { Load the block header }
   EXOS6GetBlock(40,3,ADDR(Buf[16]));
   Pic.VideoMode:=Buf[16];
   Pic.Lines:=Buf[17];
   Pic.PixelLines:=ORD(Buf[18]) MOD 2=1;
   Pic.Pal:=ORD(Buf[18])>1;
   IF (Pic.VideoMode=CHR(20)) AND NOT Pic.PixelLines THEN Pic.VideoMode:=CHR(4);
   { Put data on next segment if needed to avoid line break }
   IF (ORD(Pic.Lines)<10) AND (VOfs+ORD(Pic.Lines)*ORD(Pic.XByte)>16383) THEN
    BEGIN
     VSeg:=VSeg2; VOfs:=0;
    END;
   FOR Y0:=1 TO ORD(Pic.Lines) DO
    BEGIN
     IF Pic.PixelLines OR (Y0=1) THEN
      BEGIN
       { Read palette }
       IF Pic.Pal THEN
        IF Pic.VideoMode=CHR(50) THEN
         BEGIN
          EXOS6GetBlock(40,4,ADDR(Buf[8]));
          Buf[12]:=CHR(0);Buf[13]:=CHR(0);Buf[14]:=CHR(0);Buf[15]:=CHR(0);
         END
        ELSE
         EXOS6GetBlock(40,8,ADDR(Buf[8]));
       { Create LPB }
       NickAddr0:=(ORD(VSeg) MOD 4)*16384+VOfs;
       IF Pic.PixelLines THEN Buf[0]:=CHR(255) ELSE Buf[0]:=CHR(256-ORD(Pic.Lines));
       Buf[1]:=Pic.VideoMode;
       Buf[2]:=CHR(31-ORD(Pic.XByte) DIV 4);
       Buf[3]:=CHR(31+ORD(Pic.XByte) DIV 4);
       Buf[4]:=CHR(NickAddr0 MOD 256);
       Buf[5]:=CHR(NickAddr0 DIV 256);
       IF (Pic.VideoMode=CHR(20)) OR (Pic.VideoMode=CHR(4)) THEN
        BEGIN
         Buf[6]:=CHR((NickAddr0+ORD(Pic.XByte) DIV 2) MOD 256);
         Buf[7]:=CHR((NickAddr0+ORD(Pic.XByte) DIV 2) DIV 256);
        END;
       AddLPB;
      END;
     { Read bitmaps }
     OUT(#B3,VSeg);
     IF VOfs+ORD(Pic.XByte)<=16383 THEN
      BEGIN
       EXOS6GetBlock(40,ORD(Pic.XByte),VOfs+#C000);
       VOfs:=VOfs+ORD(Pic.XByte);
      END
     ELSE
      FOR X0:=1 TO ORD(Pic.XByte) DO
       BEGIN
        POKE(VOfs+#C000,EXOS5GetChar(40));
        IF VOfs<16383 THEN
         VOfs:=VOfs+1
        ELSE
         BEGIN
          VSeg:=VSeg2; VOfs:=0;
          OUT(#B3,VSeg);
         END;
       END;
    END;
  END;
 EXOS3CloseChn(40);
 { LPT end }
 Buf[4]:=CHR(0);Buf[5]:=CHR(0);Buf[6]:=CHR(0);Buf[7]:=CHR(0);
 Buf[8]:=CHR(0);Buf[9]:=CHR(0);Buf[10]:=CHR(0);Buf[11]:=CHR(0);Buf[12]:=CHR(0);Buf[13]:=CHR(0);Buf[14]:=CHR(0);Buf[15]:=CHR(0);
 Buf[0]:=Pic.YSize;Buf[1]:=CHR(18);Buf[2]:=CHR(63);Buf[3]:=CHR(0); AddLPB;
 Buf[0]:=CHR(236);Buf[1]:=CHR(18);Buf[2]:=CHR(63);Buf[3]:=CHR(0); AddLPB;
 Buf[0]:=CHR(252);Buf[1]:=CHR(16);Buf[2]:=CHR(6);Buf[3]:=CHR(63); AddLPB;
 Buf[0]:=CHR(255);Buf[1]:=CHR(144);Buf[2]:=CHR(63);Buf[3]:=CHR(32); AddLPB;
 Buf[0]:=CHR(252);Buf[1]:=CHR(18);Buf[2]:=CHR(6);Buf[3]:=CHR(63); AddLPB;
 Buf[0]:=CHR(255);Buf[1]:=CHR(19);Buf[2]:=CHR(63);Buf[3]:=CHR(0); AddLPB;
 SetLPTAddr((ORD(VSeg1) MOD 4)*#4000);
END;

PROCEDURE Term;
BEGIN
 SetLPTAddr(SaveLPTAddr);
 IF VSeg2<>CHR(0) THEN EXOS25FreeSeg(VSeg2);
 IF VSeg1<>CHR(0) THEN EXOS25FreeSeg(VSeg1);
 SetVar(26,0);
 SetVar(7,0);
 SetVar(8,0);
 POKE(#05BE,CHR(#28));
 POKE(#05E7,CHR(#28));
 WRITELN('ok');
END;

PROCEDURE Init;
BEGIN
 POKE(#05BE,CHR(#18));
 POKE(#05E7,CHR(#18));
 OUT(#B3,CHR(255));
 SaveLPTAddr:=PEEK(#FFF4,INTEGER)+16384;
 IF NOT GetVideoSeg THEN
  BEGIN
   WRITELN('Not enough RAM.');
   Term;
   HALT;
  END;
END;

BEGIN
 Init;
 WRITE('File name: ');
 READLN; READ(FileName.Txt);
 StrSetLen(FileName);
 IF StrPos('.',FileName)=0 THEN StrAddChrs(FileName,'.','e','p','i');
 PicLoad;
 READLN;
 Term;
END.


